{% extends "tutorial_base.html.j2" %}

{% block tutorial_body %}
        <!-- Intro Card -->
        <div class="doc-intro-card">
          <h2>Instant Version Verification, File Storage &amp; Release Notes</h2>
          <p>
            Whether you are verifying that your shop table is running the latest CAM engine update, locating your saved machine kinematics and Hypertherm cut charts to transfer them to a new shop PC, or copying system diagnostics for support, the built-in <strong>About RotaryCAM</strong> dialog and local storage directories keep your shop cutting without interruption.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-intro-grid">
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Build Provenance</span>
              <span class="value">Version, Git Commit &amp; UTC Build Date</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Local Storage</span>
              <span class="value">Machine Profiles, Cut Charts &amp; Licenses</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Support Export</span>
              <span class="value">One-Click <code class="mono">📋 Copy Diagnostics</code></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 1
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 01</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Click <code class="mono">ⓘ About</code> in the Top-Right Header Bar</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              From anywhere in the RotaryCAM workspace, look at the top-right application header bar (next to <code class="mono">⚙ Machines</code>):
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click the <span class="ui-chip">ⓘ About</span> button in the top-right navigation bar (you can also click the version badge in the bottom-right status bar).</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 1 — Clicking 'ⓘ About' in the top-right header bar</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/changelog-1-open-about.png" alt="Step 1: Clicking the About button in the top-right header bar of RotaryCAM" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 2
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 02</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Inspect Build Metadata, Release Changelog &amp; Copy Diagnostics</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              The <strong>About RotaryCAM</strong> modal opens over the workspace and is divided into three sections:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Build Metadata Table (Top):</strong> Displays the active <strong>Version</strong> (<code class="mono">v{{ version }}</code>), exact <strong>Git Commit</strong> hash, <strong>Build Date (UTC)</strong>, <strong>CI Build Run ID</strong>, host <strong>Platform</strong> (<code class="mono">darwin/arm64</code>), <strong>Build Mode</strong>, and current <strong>License Status</strong>.</li>
              <li><strong>2. What Changed — Recent Highlights (Center):</strong> A scrollable, date-stamped engineering changelog detailing newly shipped capabilities—such as <em>2D XY Sheet Nesting (VectorNester 2.0)</em>, <em>Guaranteed G94 Flat Cutting</em>, <em>Instant Nesting Profile Switching</em>, <em>5-Rule Hardware THC M63/M62 Filter &amp; HSS Sever Flat-Face Unlock</em>, <em>Maximum Plasma Amperage Machine Cap</em>, and <em>100% Offline MCP Stdio Server</em>.</li>
              <li><strong>3. Copy Diagnostics (Bottom-Right):</strong> Click <span class="ui-chip emerald">📋 Copy Diagnostics</span> to copy a formatted snapshot of your build metadata and environment to the clipboard when reporting an issue or requesting support.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 2 — The About RotaryCAM modal showing build metadata, recent changelog highlights, and Copy Diagnostics</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/changelog-2-modal.png" alt="Step 2: About RotaryCAM modal displaying build metadata, release changelog, and Copy Diagnostics button" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 3
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="storage-locations">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 03</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">System Storage &amp; Local File Locations (macOS, Windows, Linux)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              RotaryCAM keeps all machine settings, plasma charts, and project history entirely local on your machine. When you calibrate a 4-axis chuck, fine-tune cut parameters for thin-wall tubing, or nest parts across raw sticks, your data stays on your local solid-state drive—never locked behind cloud servers or lost when the shop Wi-Fi cuts out.
            </p>

            <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #fff; margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">
              What RotaryCAM Stores on Your System
            </h3>
            <p>
              Here is what each file and folder does during daily shop cutting:
            </p>

            <div style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 0.25rem 0 1rem;">
              <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.9rem; text-align: left; background: rgba(15, 20, 28, 0.6); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 8px;">
                <thead>
                  <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light); background: rgba(255,255,255,0.04);">
                    <th style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--text-muted); width: 220px;">File / Folder</th>
                    <th style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--text-muted);">Stored Information &amp; Settings</th>
                    <th style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--text-muted); width: 240px;">Shop Floor Purpose</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);">
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--amber-400);">machines.json</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top;"><strong>4-Axis Kinematics &amp; Machine Profiles:</strong> Rotary chuck gear ratios (e.g. 10:1 or 20:1), maximum motor velocities, acceleration limits, G94/G93 controller dialect, Safe Z clearance, Torch Height Control (THC) M-codes (<code class="mono">M62/M63 P1</code>), and zero-bevel defaults.</td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top; color: var(--text-secondary);">Your rotary table's motion DNA. Back this up to move your table settings to another computer.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);">
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--cyan-400);">presets.json</code><br /><code class="mono" style="color: var(--cyan-400); font-size: 0.75rem;">advanced_settings.json</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top;"><strong>Cut Charts &amp; Plasma Presets:</strong> Tuned Hypertherm Powermax SYNC cut charts, custom shop material gauges, kerf widths, cutting feedrates, arc voltages, pierce/cut heights, and pierce dwell times.</td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top; color: var(--text-secondary);">Your dialled-in cut recipes. Transfer this file so you never re-test kerf widths twice.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);">
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--emerald-400);">processed_files.json</code><br /><code class="mono" style="color: var(--emerald-400); font-size: 0.75rem;">bundles/&lt;uuid&gt;/</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top;"><strong>CAD Sessions &amp; Processed Assemblies:</strong> Recent project history, uploaded 3D STEP models, extracted parts, and compiled multi-stick nesting layouts.</td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top; color: var(--text-secondary);">Re-opens your active tubes and nests instantly across app restarts without re-importing CAD.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);">
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--amber-400);">rotarycam.lic</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top;"><strong>Offline License Token:</strong> Cryptographically signed Ed25519 offline license key that unlocks Pro features (dual-curve zero-bevel, multi-stick pack nesting, and custom kinematics).</td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top; color: var(--text-secondary);">Drop in once to unlock RotaryCAM forever on air-gapped shop tables with zero internet access.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--text-secondary);">auth_store.json</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top;"><strong>Local Auth Store:</strong> Local user session tokens and offline rate-limit status.</td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem; vertical-align: top; color: var(--text-secondary);">Keeps your signed-in account state active on the shop computer.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #fff; margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">
              Exact Local Storage Paths by Operating System
            </h3>
            <p>
              Depending on whether your shop PC runs Windows, macOS, or Linux, RotaryCAM puts its configuration files, cut charts, and license tokens in standard system application directories:
            </p>

            <!-- macOS Paths -->
            <div class="doc-code-block">
              <div class="doc-code-bar">
                <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot"></span> macOS (Shop Mac or Field Laptop)</span>
                <span class="doc-code-lang">macOS</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code># Configuration, machine profiles, cut charts &amp; session bundles
~/Library/Application Support/pipe-macos/

# Offline license drop-in (or place next to the RotaryCAM binary)
~/Library/Application Support/RotaryCAM/</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- Windows Paths -->
            <div class="doc-code-block">
              <div class="doc-code-bar">
                <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot cyan"></span> Windows (Shop Plasma Table PC)</span>
                <span class="doc-code-lang">Windows</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code># Configuration, machine profiles, cut charts &amp; session bundles
%APPDATA%\pipe-macos\
# (Typically: C:\Users\&lt;Username&gt;\AppData\Roaming\pipe-macos\)

# Offline license drop-in (or place next to RotaryCAM.exe)
%APPDATA%\RotaryCAM\
# (Typically: C:\Users\&lt;Username&gt;\AppData\Roaming\RotaryCAM\)</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- Linux Paths -->
            <div class="doc-code-block">
              <div class="doc-code-bar">
                <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot emerald"></span> Linux (LinuxCNC &amp; Industrial Touch Controllers)</span>
                <span class="doc-code-lang">Linux</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code># Configuration, machine profiles, cut charts &amp; session bundles
~/.config/pipe-macos/
# (Or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pipe-macos/)

# Offline license drop-in (or place next to the rotarycam binary)
~/.config/RotaryCAM/
# (Or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/RotaryCAM/)</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- Callout: Backing Up Machine Profiles & Presets -->
            <div class="doc-callout tip" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">✓ How to Back Up &amp; Transfer Profiles to a New Plasma Table PC</div>
              <p>
                When switching shop computers or setting up a second rotary table, you do not have to re-enter gear ratios or re-tune torch cut speeds by hand:
              </p>
              <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li><strong>1. Plug in a USB thumb drive:</strong> Open the <code class="mono">pipe-macos</code> folder on your old PC (see paths above).</li>
                <li><strong>2. Copy your tuned files:</strong> Copy <code class="mono">machines.json</code> (your chuck ratios, motor velocities, and Safe Z limits) and <code class="mono">presets.json</code> (your dialled-in kerf widths, feedrates, and torch height voltages).</li>
                <li><strong>3. Paste into the new PC:</strong> Install RotaryCAM on the new machine, paste both files into its <code class="mono">pipe-macos</code> folder, and launch RotaryCAM. All your machine kinematics and plasma charts are ready to cut immediately.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- Callout: Air-Gapped Offline License Drop-in -->
            <div class="doc-callout info" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">🔑 Offline License Activation (Air-Gapped Shop Tables)</div>
              <p>
                Rotary plasma tables often sit in metal shop buildings with spotty Wi-Fi or on dedicated air-gapped machine computers with zero internet access. RotaryCAM never requires online activation or recurring server pings:
              </p>
              <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li><strong>Drop-in location:</strong> Copy your signed <code class="mono">rotarycam.lic</code> license file into the <code class="mono">RotaryCAM</code> application support folder (or place it directly in the same folder as the RotaryCAM executable).</li>
                <li><strong>Immediate unlock:</strong> RotaryCAM checks the cryptographic Ed25519 signature locally in under 5 milliseconds on startup, unlocking zero-bevel saddle compensation, multi-stick nesting, and custom kinematics forever without touching the internet.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             RELEASE LOG
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="release-transcript">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge" style="background: rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.15); color: var(--cyan-400); border-color: rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.35);">LOG</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Current Release Changelog Entries</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p style="color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
              The built-in changelog view reflects all recent production engine updates. Below is the full transcript of recent engineering releases directly from the application:
            </p>

            <!-- Latest Release: Oct 30 (v0.3.1) -->
            <div style="background: rgba(20, 23, 29, 0.95); border: 1px solid var(--surface-border); border-left: 3px solid var(--plasma-amber); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
              <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.85rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06); padding-bottom: 0.6rem;">
                <span class="mono" style="color: var(--plasma-amber); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.95rem;">🗓️ Oct 30, 2026 (v0.3.1)</span>
                <span style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.82rem;">Full-Speed G93 Indexing, Contiguous Snake A-Axis Cutout Ordering, Optional #1111/#1112 Outputs &amp; 1&Prime;&times;1&Prime; HSS Sever THC Unlock</span>
              </div>
              <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li><strong>Full-Speed <code class="mono">G93</code> Inter-Feature Indexing:</strong> Native <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time inter-feature indexing moves now run at full machine <code class="mono">maxVelA</code> and <code class="mono">rapidRate</code> limits instead of the non-G93 <em>Safe Rotation</em> feedrate (<code class="mono">interFeatureRotationFeedRate</code>), and <em>Safe Rotation (&deg;/min)</em> in Machine Settings is automatically disabled when <code class="mono">G93</code> mode is active.</li>
                <li><strong>Contiguous Snake A-Axis Cutout Ordering:</strong> Co-axial internal cutouts at the same X station sequence along a contiguous circular chain and alternate (snake) direction across stations, eliminating 180&deg; cross-face jumps and cumulative negative A-axis wind-up.</li>
                <li><strong>Optional Pre-Pierce Amperage (<code class="mono">#1111</code>) &amp; Gas Pressure (<code class="mono">#1112</code>) Outputs:</strong> Added per-machine <em>Emit Amperage (<code class="mono">#1111</code>)</em> and <em>Emit Gas Pressure (<code class="mono">#1112</code>)</em> checkboxes under <em>Machine Settings &rarr; Dynamic Parameter Modulation</em> (defaulting to off) so pre-pierce RS485 parameters are only emitted when enabled.</li>
                <li><strong>1&Prime;&times;1&Prime; HSS Sever Cut Flat-Face THC Unlock at High Cut Speeds:</strong> Corner-approach deceleration ramps on rectangular and square HSS tubes no longer double-lock straight flat faces outside the geometric corner window, keeping hardware THC unlocked (<code class="mono">M62 P0</code>) across all 3 full flat faces on 1&Prime;&times;1&Prime; (<code class="mono">25.4&times;25.4 mm</code>) HSS <code class="mono">Startcut</code> and <code class="mono">Endcut</code> severs even at high plasma cutting speeds.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- Oct 29 Release (v0.3.0) -->
            <div style="background: rgba(20, 23, 29, 0.95); border: 1px solid var(--surface-border); border-left: 3px solid var(--cyan-arc); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
              <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.85rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06); padding-bottom: 0.6rem;">
                <span class="mono" style="color: var(--cyan-arc); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.95rem;">🗓️ Oct 29, 2026 (v0.3.0)</span>
                <span style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.82rem;">2D XY Sheet Nesting (VectorNester 2.0), Guaranteed G94 Flat Cutting, Instant Nesting Profile Switching, Max Amperage Cap &amp; HSS Sever THC Unlock</span>
              </div>
              <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li><strong>2D XY Sheet Nesting (VectorNester 2.0):</strong> Multi-Part Pack Nesting now packs flat bar and sheet parts (<code class="mono">Profile_Flat</code>) across configurable <em>Sheet Length (X)</em> and <em>Sheet Width (Y)</em> stock using adaptive guillotine remnant optimization, obstacle-relative edge matching, and Part-in-Hole interior cavity nesting with automatic inside-out containment cut ordering. Includes an interactive 2D SVG sheet layout preview showing part perimeters, interior holes/cutouts, and 90&deg; rotations, plus exact 3D simulator sheet dimensions.</li>
                <li><strong>Guaranteed <code class="mono">G94</code> Feedrate Mode on Flat Stock:</strong> Flat stock always outputs standard <code class="mono">G94</code> units-per-minute feedrates in both single-part and pack mode&mdash;even when your active machine profile is configured for <code class="mono">G93</code> rotary inverse-time mode, so linear XY plate cutting always runs in mm/min or IPM.</li>
                <li><strong>Instant Nesting Profile Switching &amp; In-Flight G-Code Cancellation:</strong> Switching cutting profiles or editing nesting quantities in Multi-Part Pack Nesting immediately aborts any in-flight G-code compilation and replaces pending UI swaps (<code class="mono">hx-sync</code>) so your workspace responds instantaneously.</li>
                <li><strong>Flat Profile 3D Cut Contour Projection &amp; Profile Override Reset:</strong> Cutting contours, lead-ins, and lead-outs on flat bar and plate parts (<code class="mono">Profile_Flat</code>) project directly onto the flat 3D plate surface instead of wrapping around a cylinder, plus a one-click <code class="mono">&#x21BA; Reset</code> button on Profile Override restores the originally detected profile.</li>
                <li><strong>Hardware THC Unlock Across All HSS Sever Cut Flat Faces:</strong> Unlocks hardware THC (<code class="mono">M62 P0</code>) cleanly across every flat face on rectangular and square HSS sever cuts (<code class="mono">Endcut</code> / <code class="mono">Startcut</code>, down to 1&Prime;&times;1&Prime; / 25.4&times;25.4 mm tubes) by preventing 4-axis rotary corner tangency vertices and slowdown/breakout segments from double-padding adjacent straight runs.</li>
                <li><strong>Max Plasma Amperage Machine Setting (<code class="mono">maxAmperage</code>):</strong> Set your plasma power supply's rated limit (45A, 65A, 85A, or 105A) under <em>Machine Settings &rarr; THC &amp; Plasma Hardware</em>. Automatic Hypertherm SYNC cartridge selection caps recommendations to your machine's maximum amperage, disables oversized cartridges in the session selector, and warns if a selected cartridge exceeds your power supply.</li>
                <li><strong>Single-Default Machine Profile &amp; Optional Post-<code class="mono">M5</code> Unwind Dwell:</strong> Enforces a single default machine profile that automatically applies whenever you load a new STEP file, and makes the post-<code class="mono">M5</code> Z-axis unwind dwell (<code class="mono">G4 P200</code>) optional and disabled by default so machines that do not need a post-cut dwell retract immediately.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- Oct 26 Release -->
            <div style="background: rgba(20, 23, 29, 0.95); border: 1px solid var(--surface-border); border-left: 3px solid var(--cyan-arc); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
              <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.85rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06); padding-bottom: 0.6rem;">
                <span class="mono" style="color: var(--cyan-arc); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.95rem;">🗓️ Oct 26, 2026</span>
                <span style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.82rem;">5-Rule Hardware THC M63/M62 Filter, Per-Profile Preamble Macros &amp; One-Direction Cut Ordering</span>
              </div>
              <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li><strong>Per-Profile Closed-Stock Preamble Macros &amp; <code class="mono">#1120</code> Material Width:</strong> Optional startup preamble macros for Round Tube (<code class="mono">roundPreambleMacro</code>) and Rectangular/Square HSS (<code class="mono">rectangularPreambleMacro</code>) in Machine Settings that assign <code class="mono">#1120</code> to the material width at <code class="mono">A=0</code> before running your ohmic torch-centering routine.</li>
                <li><strong>One-Direction (Monotonic <code class="mono">X</code>) Cut Ordering by Default:</strong> Disabled Thermal Hedging by default across built-in machine profiles so stock feeds in one direction along <code class="mono">X</code> out of the chuck and support rollers. You can still turn Thermal Hedging on in the sidebar or Machine Settings for heat-sensitive thin-wall tubing.</li>
                <li><strong>5-Rule Hardware THC <code class="mono">M63</code>/<code class="mono">M62</code> Post-Processor Filter:</strong> Deterministic multi-pass filter over <code class="mono">G1</code> segments that locks THC (<code class="mono">M63 P0</code>) on lead-ins/outs, sharp deflections (&gt;10&deg;) or tight radii (<code class="mono">R &lt; 15 mm</code>), 4-axis tube corner rotations, feedrate drops (&lt;85% target speed), and pierce crater proximity (&le;5 mm), with acceleration/deceleration padding and straight-run hysteresis so THC only unlocks on settled flat runs.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- Oct 23 Release -->
            <div style="background: rgba(20, 23, 29, 0.95); border: 1px solid var(--surface-border); border-left: 3px solid var(--cyan-arc); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
              <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.85rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06); padding-bottom: 0.6rem;">
                <span class="mono" style="color: var(--cyan-arc); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.95rem;">🗓️ Oct 23, 2026 (v0.2.4)</span>
                <span style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.82rem;">100% Offline MCP Stdio Server, Drive Physics Tools &amp; Bent HSS Preview Alignment</span>
              </div>
              <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li><strong>100% Offline Model Context Protocol (MCP) Stdio Server (<code class="mono">rotarycam-mcp</code>):</strong> Standalone, 100% offline MCP server binary for macOS (arm64), Linux (x64), and Windows (x64) bundled directly with RotaryCAM. Connect Claude Desktop, Cursor, or local open-weights models to configure machines, analyze STEP files, apply zero-bevel compensation, and generate production G-code using your local <code class="mono">rotarycam.lic</code> without sending a single byte to the cloud.</li>
